iOS won't let a regular app intercept and rewrite URLs inside the YouTube app or Safari automatically — there's no built-in redirect rule for that. What actually works:

**Safari (for links opened in Safari)**
Use a userscript/extension manager like **Userscripts** (free, App Store) or **Stay**. Add a script that rewrites `youtube.com/shorts/VIDEOID` to `youtube.com/watch?v=VIDEOID`:

```js
// ==UserScript==
// @name        Shorts to Watch
// @match       *://*.youtube.com/*
// @run-at      document-start
// ==/UserScript==
if (location.pathname.startsWith('/shorts/')) {
  const id = location.pathname.split('/')[2];
  location.replace('https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=' + id);
}
```

**Caveat:** this only fires inside Safari. When you tap a Short in the YouTube *app*, nothing outside the app can touch the URL — iOS gives no hook for that.

**Practical workarounds for the app:**
- Long-press the Short → **Copy link**, then paste into Safari (your script converts it).
- Or just avoid the app and use Safari for YouTube, where the script handles every Short automatically.

If you want a no-script option, some third-party YouTube clients (e.g. Yattee) let you disable Shorts entirely — but that's a different app, not a redirect.

