Clean Architecture in Express means separating your app into layers so business logic doesn't depend on Express, the database, or any framework. Express becomes a thin delivery mechanism at the edge.

The usual four layers:

- **Domain** — entities and business rules. Plain JavaScript/TypeScript, no imports from Express or a DB.
- **Application** — use cases. Orchestrates domain logic, defines interfaces (ports) for what it needs.
- **Infrastructure** — concrete implementations: Express routes, Prisma/Postgres repos, email clients.
- **Interface adapters** — controllers that translate HTTP requests into use-case calls and back.

The dependency rule: inner layers know nothing about outer ones. Domain imports nothing. Use cases depend on interfaces, not implementations.

A quick sketch:

```ts
// domain/user.ts
export class User {
  constructor(public id: string, public email: string) {}
}

// application/ports.ts
export interface UserRepo {
  save(user: User): Promise<void>;
  findByEmail(email: string): Promise<User | null>;
}

// application/registerUser.ts
export const registerUser = (repo: UserRepo) => async (email: string) => {
  if (await repo.findByEmail(email)) throw new Error("exists");
  await repo.save(new User(crypto.randomUUID(), email));
};

// infrastructure/express.ts
app.post("/users", async (req, res) => {
  try {
    await registerUser(pgUserRepo)(req.body.email);
    res.status(201).end();
  } catch (e) {
    res.status(400).json({ error: (e as Error).message });
  }
});
```

**Trade-off:** this is real overhead for a small CRUD app. It pays off when business rules are complex, when you might swap the DB or framework, or when you want to test use cases without spinning up Express. If none of those apply, a simpler structure is fine — don't add layers for their own sake.

