The main tool in Kendo UI is `kendo.destroy()` — call it on a container to recursively destroy every widget inside it. Widgets that aren't destroyed keep listening to events and holding references, which is the usual source of leaks in a SPA.

In a Kendo UI SPA, do the cleanup in each view's `hide` (or `beforeHide`) handler:

```javascript
var viewModel = kendo.observable({
  hide: function () {
    kendo.destroy(this.element);
  }
});
```

Other things that matter:

- Destroy widgets you create outside views (grids, windows, dropdowns) with `widget.destroy()` when you remove them.
- Unbind your own handlers (`$(el).off(...)`, `widget.unbind(...)`) — `destroy()` handles Kendo's internal ones, not yours.
- Remove DOM nodes you added with plain jQuery/JS; Kendo won't know about them.
- Watch out for closures and timers holding references to removed elements.

To find leaks, use Chrome DevTools: take a heap snapshot, navigate away, force GC, take another, and compare retained `kendo.ui.Widget` instances or detached nodes.

I'm not certain of the exact API for every widget version, so check the Kendo docs for the specific components you're using.

Dependency injection is when you stop lettin' your classes go out and score their own dependencies, and instead you hand 'em what they need from the outside. No more `new`-ing up your own crew inside the joint.

Say you got a `BankAccount` that needs a `Logger`. The amateur move:

```csharp
public class BankAccount
{
    private readonly Logger _logger = new Logger(); // snitchin' on yourself
}
```

Now `BankAccount` is married to `Logger` forever. Can't swap it, can't test it without draggin' the real thing along.

The made-man move — pass it in:

```csharp
public class BankAccount
{
    private readonly ILogger _logger;
    public BankAccount(ILogger logger) => _logger = logger;
}
```

Now whoever runs the operation decides what `ILogger` gets handed over. In .NET you wire it up in the container:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<ILogger, FileLogger>();
builder.Services.AddScoped<BankAccount>();
```

The container's the fixer. It sees `BankAccount` needs an `ILogger`, so it supplies one. You just ask for the `BankAccount` and everything's already connected.

Why it matters: you can slip in a fake logger for tests, swap `FileLogger` for `CloudLogger` without touchin' `BankAccount`, and each class only worries about its own business. Loose coupling, clean hands.

