Use `java.security.MessageDigest` with the algorithm name `"MD5"`. Stream the whole file through it, then format the digest as hex.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.security.MessageDigest;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        try (InputStream in = new FileInputStream("example.txt")) {
            byte[] buf = new byte[8192];
            int n;
            while ((n = in.read(buf)) != -1) {
                md.update(buf, 0, n);
            }
        }
        byte[] hash = md.digest();

        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (byte b : hash) {
            sb.append(String.format("%02x", b));
        }
        System.out.println(sb.toString());
    }
}
```

Two things worth knowing:

- There is no `java.util.MD5` class — only `MessageDigest`.
- MD5 is cryptographically broken (collisions are easy to produce). It's fine for checksums against accidental corruption, but for security use SHA-256 instead — just change `"MD5"` to `"SHA-256"`.

For large files, the streaming loop above matters: reading the whole file into one array will fail on big inputs.

The problem isn't the variable's value — it's that a controller (or component) is the wrong place to store it. Angular re-creates components whenever you navigate, so anything stored on a component is lost. State that needs to survive navigation belongs in a **service**, which Angular creates once and shares via dependency injection.

```typescript
// auth.service.ts
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { BehaviorSubject } from 'rxjs';

@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class AuthService {
  private userSubject = new BehaviorSubject<User | null>(null);
  user$ = this.userSubject.asObservable();

  login(user: User) { this.userSubject.next(user); }
  logout() { this.userSubject.next(null); }
}
```

Then inject it wherever you need it:

```typescript
// navbar.component.ts
constructor(private auth: AuthService) {}
```

```html
<nav *ngIf="auth.user$ | async as user">
  <!-- nav items based on user.role -->
</nav>
```

For route access control, use a `CanActivate` guard that reads the same service. `providedIn: 'root'` makes it a singleton, so nothing resets on navigation. If you want the login to survive a page refresh, persist the user to `localStorage` in the service.

(One note: your question mentions controllers and scope, which is AngularJS syntax. If you're on AngularJS rather than Angular 2+, the same idea applies but the syntax is a `.service()` or `.factory()` instead.)

