`List` is an interface, not a class, so you can't create one directly. You need a concrete implementation like `ArrayList`:

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

List<String> supplierNames = new ArrayList<>();
supplierNames.add("sup1");
supplierNames.add("sup2");
supplierNames.add("sup3");
System.out.println(supplierNames.get(1)); // sup2
```

Two things to note: declare the variable as `List<String>` (the interface) but instantiate `ArrayList` (the implementation). The `<String>` part is also needed — plain `List` without a type argument is a raw type and will warn or error depending on your settings.

If `List` isn't recognized at all, add `import java.util.List;` at the top of your file.

