Here's a working approach. The core idea: try to open the app via a custom URL scheme, and fall back to the store if nothing happens.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Opening app…</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; text-align: center; padding: 40px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <p id="msg">Opening the app…</p>

  <script>
    const ua = navigator.userAgent || navigator.vendor || window.opera;
    const isIOS = /iPad|iPhone|iPod/.test(ua) && !window.MSStream;
    const isAndroid = /android/i.test(ua);

    // Custom scheme your app registers
    const scheme = "myapp://open";
    const iosStore = "https://apps.apple.com/app/idXXXXXXXX";
    const androidStore = "https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.example.app";

    function openApp() {
      if (isIOS) {
        // iOS: location assignment works; fall back to store after a delay
        const t = Date.now();
        window.location.href = scheme;
        setTimeout(() => {
          if (Date.now() - t < 2000) window.location.href = iosStore;
        }, 1500);
      } else if (isAndroid) {
        // Android: intent:// with a store fallback is the reliable path
        const intent = `intent://open#Intent;scheme=myapp;package=com.example.app;` +
                       `S.browser_fallback_url=${encodeURIComponent(androidStore)};end`;
        window.location.href = intent;
      } else {
        document.getElementById("msg").textContent = "Open this page on your phone.";
      }
    }

    openApp();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

Key points and caveats:

- **Custom URL schemes are unreliable on modern browsers.** iOS Safari may show a permission dialog; Chrome blocks scheme navigation from some contexts. There's no way to *detect* whether the app is installed from the web — you can only try and fall back.
- **Android `intent://`** is the most reliable mechanism, with `S.browser_fallback_url` handling the "not installed" case natively.
- **iOS Universal Links / Android App Links** are the proper solution: a link to your real domain that opens the app if installed, else the website. Requires server config (`apple-app-site-association` / `assetlinks.json`) and app-side setup. If you control the app, prefer these over custom schemes.
- Replace the scheme, package name, and store URLs with your own.

